  "summary": "Six preprints test what biological foundation models encode and whether simpler or structured designs match them, using annotations, perturbations and encoders.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is scrutiny of what learned biological representations contain, rather than accuracy alone. Sparse autoencoder features from transcriptomic foundation models largely behave as gene detectors on spatial panels, and interpretability conclusions for ESMC protein language models depend on residue-level curated annotations. A 4-D value track and simple encodings retain most performance in single-cell foundation models, questioning 512-dimensional value embeddings. Other work builds structure in: CIRCE uses a Boltzmann distribution over Hamming distance in combinatorial perturbation space to infer input identity and graded signaling strength, and CMC-VAE links genes and proteins causally in CITE-seq data, with interventions refining perturbation predictions. SimpleP2G compares image encoders for predicting expression from pathology images, favoring Virchow2 features with a simple frozen-embedding decoder.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
